N001:{s:"There is a finite A=A(T,gamma,R), uniform for all real mean-zero initial data g with ||g||_{H^gamma}<=R, such that u_t+u_xxx=(1/2)partial_x(u^2), u(0)=g, has a unique real mean-zero C([0,T];H^gamma) solution obtained by smooth approximation, distributionally solving the equation, with sup_t||u(t)||_{H^gamma}<=A. Smooth initial data have smooth solutions. Set v(t)=S_{-t}u(t); then v'=B_t(v,v)/2 and ||v(t)||_{H^gamma}=||u(t)||_{H^gamma}. For two such flows, or smooth approximations, whose initial L2 norms sum to at most B0, sup_t||u-tilde u||_2<=C(T,B0)||g-tilde g||_2. Thus M=1+sup_t||v(t)||_{H^gamma}<=1+A uniformly.",ds:[d.H,d.S,d.B],bb:[["g","real mean-zero H^gamma datum"],["u","corresponding solution"],["v","twisted solution"],["A","finite uniform bound"],["M","one plus the exact solution supremum"],["B0","nonnegative initial L2 sum bound"],["tilde g","second datum"],["tilde u","second solution"],["t","time in [0,T]"]]},
N002:{s:"For every tau>0 and real alpha,beta, eta_tau(alpha,beta)=0 if alpha beta=0, and otherwise |eta|<=2 min(|alpha/beta|,|beta/alpha|) and |eta|<=tau min(|alpha|,|beta|). For any nonzero integers a,b,c,d with zero sum, put A=|ab|, B0=|cd| and let N1>=N2>=N3>=N4 be their sorted magnitudes. Then N1<=3N2, |d|^(-1)min(A/B0,B0/A)<=3/N3 and |a+b|/max(A,B0)<=6/N3.",ds:[d.eta,d.N3],bb:[["tau","positive real"],["alpha","real"],["beta","real"],...["a","b","c","d"].map(x=>[x,"nonzero integer with a+b+c+d=0"]),["A","|ab|"],["B0","|cd|"],...["N1","N2","N4"].map(x=>[x,"sorted frequency magnitude"])]},
N003:{s:"For any four nonnegative sequences x1,x2,x3,x4 in ell2(Z excluding zero), sum_{a+b+c+d=0,abcd!=0} x1(a)x2(b)x3(c)x4(d)/N3(a,b,c,d)<=C product_{r=1}^4||xr||_ell2, with an absolute constant C. The same bound holds after restricting to any subset of the interactions, and the infinite sum means the supremum of its finite nonnegative partial sums.",ds:[d.N3,d.H],bb:["x1","x2","x3","x4"].map(x=>[x,"nonnegative ell2 sequence"])},
N004:{s:"For every lambda in [0,1], tau>0 and real step time t_n, R2 has a unique bounded trilinear extension from mean-zero H^lambda inputs to mean-zero L2 and ||R^n_{2,tau}(f1,f2,f3)||_2<=C tau^(1+lambda) product_j||fj||_{H^lambda}, uniformly in t_n,tau and support. This holds with any multiplier of modulus <=1 in its defining sum, hence on Phi=0 and Phi!=0 separately. The endpoint multiplier bound is |eta_tau(alpha,beta)|/(|k||abc|^lambda)<=6^(1-lambda)18^lambda tau^lambda/N3(a,b,c,-k) for nonzero abck and a+b!=0. In particular, for mean-zero w in H^gamma, mean-zero f in L2, ||w||_{H^gamma}<=M and delta=||f-w||_2<=1, and 0<tau<=1, ||R^n_{2,tau}(f,f,f)||_2<=C_M tau(tau^gamma+delta).",ds:[d.R2,d.N3,d.H],bb:[["lambda","real exponent in [0,1]"],["tau","positive real"],["t_n","real time"],...["f1","f2","f3"].map(x=>[x,"mean-zero H^lambda input"]),["w","mean-zero H^gamma function"],["f","mean-zero L2 function"],["M","bound for ||w||_H^gamma"],["delta","||f-w||_2"],["a","integer"],["b","integer"],["c","integer"],["k","a+b+c"],["alpha","3ab(a+b)"],["beta","3kc(a+b)"],["Phi","k^3-a^3-b^3-c^3"]]},
N005:{s:"With normalized norms, for every I=[a,b] subset [0,T], 0<=b-a<=1, and three functions fj in X(I), ||C_I(f1,f2,f3)||_2<=C|I| product_j||fj||_X+C||fi||_{L-infinity H^(-pstar)} product_{j!=i}||fj||_{L-infinity L2} for each i=1,2,3; for time-independent fj in H^lambda, 0<=lambda<=1, ||C_I(f1,f2,f3)||_2<=C_lambda |I|^lambda product_j||fj||_{H^lambda}. The first assertion may in particular be used on continuous, spatially smooth, piecewise time-smooth inputs. For 0<tau<=1 and mean-zero w with ||w||_{H^gamma}<=M, sup_{I_n}||F_n(t;w)||_{H^{a_gamma}}<=C_M tau^gamma and sup_{I_n}||F_n(t;w)||_{H^{1+gamma}}<=C_M. Also ||fg||_2<=C_gamma ||f||_{H^gamma}||g||_{H^{a_gamma}} for f,g in these spaces. All constants are independent of step location and support.",ds:[d.CI,d.X,d.Fn,d.ag],bb:[["I","compact interval"],["a","left time"],["b","right time"],...["f1","f2","f3"].map(x=>[x,"time-dependent spatial function"]),["lambda","exponent in [0,1]"],["i","slot index in {1,2,3}"],["tau","step size"],["I_n","[t_n,t_n+tau] subset [0,T]"],["t_n","step start"],["w","mean-zero H^gamma input"],["M","H^gamma bound"],["t","step time"],["f","H^gamma function"],["g","H^{a_gamma} function"]]},
N006:{s:"For all real t and mean-zero L2 f,g, ||Dinv f||_{H^1}+||Dinv f||_infinity<=C||f||_2, ||B_t(f,g)||_{H^(-pstar)}<=C||f||_2||g||_2, ||J_t(f,g)||_{H^1}<=C||f||_2||g||_2, and ||J_t(f,g)||_2<=C||Dinv f||_2||g||_2. For arbitrary L2 F,G, ||Dinv P(FG)||_2<=C||F||_2||G||_2. For mean-zero A with Dinv A in L2, ||A||_{H^(-pstar)}<=||Dinv A||_2. H^1 is an algebra, and ||AB||_{H^1}<=C||A||_{H^1}||B||_{H^1}. Each S_t is a Sobolev isometry and preserves reality and mean. Fourier projections contract every Sobolev norm and X; for N>=1, ||P_{>N}f||_{H^{s0}}<=C N^(s0-s)||f||_{H^s} and ||P_{<=N}f||_{H^s}<=C N^(s-s0)||f||_{H^{s0}} for s>=s0. For smooth mean-zero time-dependent f,g on [a,b], integral_a^b B_t(f,g)dt=(1/3)[J_t(f,g)]_a^b-(1/3)integral_a^b[J_t(f',g)+J_t(f,g')]dt. J is symmetric and integral_I J_t(B_t(f,g),h)dt=C_I(f,g,h). These integral identities hold for continuous piecewise time-smooth inputs by summing over their pieces.",ds:[d.H,d.S,d.Dinv,d.P,d.B,d.J,d.CI,d.X],bb:[["t","real time"],...["f","g","h","F","G","A","B"].map(x=>[x,"function or distribution with the domain specified in the corresponding clause"]),["N","real Fourier threshold >=1"],["s","real Sobolev exponent"],["s0","real Sobolev exponent <=s"],["a","left time"],["b","right time"],["I","time interval"]]},
N007:{s:"For every smooth real mean-zero datum u0 and positive integer L with tau=T/L>0, the finite scheme iterates are smooth, real and mean-zero. The interpolation V from D027 is continuous on [0,T], piecewise time-smooth and spatially smooth, and V(t_n)=v^n=S_{-t_n}u^n. On every step with f=v^n, v^{n+1}=f+F_n(t_{n+1};f)+C_n(t_{n+1};f)-R^n_{2,tau}(f,f,f). Moreover F_n'=B_t(f,f)/2, V'=B_t(z_n,z_n)/2-W_n/tau, and Y_n'=B_t(f,F_n)+B_t(F_n,F_n)/2-W_n/tau, with Y_n(t_n)=0.",ds:[d.Psi,d.V,d.Y,d.z,d.W,d.Fn,d.Cn,d.R2],bb:[["L","positive integer"],["tau","T/L"],["n","step index"],["t_n","n tau"],["t","step time"],["f","v^n"]]},
