{text:"H001 supplies the positive horizon and 0<gamma<=1. Apply E001 with s=gamma, horizon T, initial L2 bound R and initial dot H^gamma bound R, to arbitrary real mean-zero data in that ball. The source has normalized measure. It gives a bound uniform over these bounded norms and a solution. Its uniqueness/continuity branch is 5.1 for gamma>1/2 and 6.3 for 0<gamma<=1/2. The source theta=0 estimate gives uniform L2 dependence with initial sum bound B0. These applications concern only exact flows, not scheme convergence.",uses:["H001","E001"],fact:{id:"F001",statement:"For the source sign of KdV, the real mean-zero H^gamma ball has a uniform finite homogeneous Sobolev bound and a unique continuous flow; pairs have the E001 theta=0 L2 dependence bound. Smooth data give smooth flows by applying the same existence and uniqueness results at every integer Sobolev exponent."}},
{text:range(80,80)+" More explicitly, the physical change u(t,x)=-U(t,-x) maps initial data g to source initial data -g(-x), so preserves the whole initial-data ball. The homogeneous-to-inhomogeneous inequalities follow coefficientwise from |k|<=<k><=sqrt(2)|k| for k!=0. For a continuous limiting flow the finite-frequency sums and then their supremum give the bound at each time, with the same constant. Apply the source pair bound after this isometry; it gives the stated C(T,B0) bound. Twisting by S_{-t} differentiates away -partial_x^3 and gives v'=B_t(v,v)/2. This proves all clauses of N001, for arbitrary data in the ball.",local:["F001"]}
