{text:"Use H001. For E002 take arbitrary I=[a,b] subset [0,T] with b-a<=1 and lambda=0 for the X estimate. X gives precisely L-infinity L2 and L-infinity H^(-23/14) derivative, its hypotheses. Its three choices of negative-norm slot are retained. For constant inputs use E002 with any lambda in [0,1]. Each source spatial norm is sqrt(2 pi) times ours: divide by sqrt(2 pi) on the output and absorb the remaining product factors 2 pi into the constant. This proves the first two assertions without changing their exponent or time length. Smooth or continuous piecewise smooth time functions have the stated distributional derivative, so E002 applies directly. General X inputs use the source bounded interpretation of the operator.",uses:["H001","E002"],fact:["F001","The normalized time-dependent trilinear estimate holds with each negative-norm slot, and the normalized time-independent estimate holds for every lambda in [0,1], as stated in N005."]},
{text:"For the product and stages use E003 with lambda=gamma and epsilon=1/8 at gamma=1/2. The diagonal bilinear stage is exactly F_n(t;w), by definition. The necessary source exponent checks and retained calculations are:\n"+ch.r5.replace(/\[1, Eqs\. \(2\.2\)–\(2\.3\)\]/g,"E003").replace(/\(17\)/g,"the asserted product inequality").replace(/\(18\)/g,"the asserted stage inequalities")+"\nFor the first range the exponent inequality reduces to (1-gamma)(1/2-gamma)>=0; at gamma=1/2 the exponent is 11/17 and in the last range (1+gamma)/2>=gamma. Thus both stage conclusions follow uniformly for 0<tau<=1. All relevant bounds and the product inequality have now been proved in the declared normalization.",uses:["E003"],local:["F001"]}
