make(6,[],"N006",[d.H,d.S,d.P,d.Dinv,d.B,d.J,d.CI,d.X],[
{text:"Fix functions in the classes stated in the output; retain those conditions as L1. Orthogonality of finite Fourier polynomials gives Parseval, hence the completion identifies H^0 with normalized L2. The multiplier exp(itk^3) has modulus one, so S_t is an isometry. Its conjugate relation at -k proves preservation of reality, and its zero coefficient proves preservation of the mean. Every Fourier projection deletes nonnegative terms of squared Sobolev sums and commutes with time derivatives, so contracts Sobolev norms and both terms of X. Comparing <k> powers on |k|<=N and |k|>N gives the two stated Bernstein inequalities, with fixed constants for N>=1. Cauchy–Schwarz gives ||Dinv f||_infinity<=(sum_{k!=0}k^(-2))^(1/2)||f||_2 and ||Dinv f||_{H^1}<=sqrt(2)||f||_2. The analogous Fourier estimate gives ||A0||_infinity<=C||A0||_{H^1}. The product rule, placing the undifferentiated factor in L-infinity in each term, gives ||A0 B0||_{H^1}<=C||A0||_{H^1}||B0||_{H^1}. Thus J_t has the asserted H^1 bound. Also ||J_t(f,g)||_2<=||S_t Dinv f||_2||S_t Dinv g||_infinity<=C||Dinv f||_2||g||_2. Density extends these products from polynomials.",fact:["F001","S_t, the Fourier projections, the inverse derivative and H^1 products satisfy the asserted isometry, projection, Bernstein, inverse-derivative and J estimates."]},
{text:"For L2 F,G the convolution coefficient bound |(FG)hat(k)|<=||F||_2||G||_2 follows by Cauchy–Schwarz. Hence ||B_t(f,g)||_{H^(-pstar)}^2<=||f||_2^2||g||_2^2 sum_{k!=0}k^2<k>^(-2pstar). This series is finite because pstar=23/14>3/2. The same coefficient bound with sum_{k!=0}k^(-2) yields ||Dinv P(FG)||_2<=C||F||_2||G||_2. For mean-zero A, <k>^(-pstar)<=|k|^(-1) for k!=0 because pstar>1, giving its claimed negative-norm inequality. All constants are independent of time by F001.",local:["F001"],fact:["F002","The asserted B negative-norm bound, Dinv P product bound and negative-norm comparison hold."]},
{text:"For smooth mean-zero time-dependent f,g, J_t(f,g) has coefficient sum_{a0+b0=k} exp(-3itka0b0)(ia0)^(-1)(ib0)^(-1) fhat(a0)ghat(b0) at k!=0. Differentiate: the phase derivative is 3ik times the product coefficient, so partial_t J_t(f,g)=3 B_t(f,g)+J_t(f',g)+J_t(f,g'). Integrating proves the stated normal form, retaining both endpoints. The sums of smooth functions and all needed derivatives converge absolutely, justifying differentiation and integration. Symmetry is immediate from the product. Since Dinv partial_x=P, substitution gives J_t(B_t(f,g),h)=S_{-t}P{P[(S_tf)(S_tg)]S_t Dinv h}; integrate to get C_I. For a continuous piecewise smooth pair, apply on each piece; adjacent boundary values cancel, leaving both outer endpoints. No delta mass appears in its distributional derivative. Discharge L1, retaining each specified function class in its universally quantified clause.",local:["F001","F002"]}
