{text:"For each fixed tau>0, Psi_tau is continuous L2->L2. In fact N006 gives Dinv:L2->H^1 and the H^1 algebra bound, so F_tau:L2->H^1 is continuous quadratic. The first H_tau term is the product of S_tau Dinv f and Dinv F_tau(f), hence continuous into L2. The second uses the continuous scalar bilinear mean P0(f^2), whose modulus is bounded by ||f||_2^2, times S_tau Dinv f. In the third, each endpoint cube (S_s Dinv f)^3 is a continuous H^1 product followed by Dinv. The fourth endpoint has S_{s-tau} Dinv2 F_tau(f) and S_s Dinv f, again H^1 factors, followed by Dinv2. The Airy group is an isometry, and 1/tau is finite for this fixed step. Thus all terms and their finite iterates are continuous. They preserve reality and zero mean as in their displayed formulas. For fixed L and tau=T/L, finite induction gives u_m^n->u^n in L2 at every n=0,...,L.",uses:["N006"],local:["F001"],fact:["F002","For every fixed tau>0, the unfiltered Psi_tau is continuous on L2 and has well-defined real mean-zero iterates. On a fixed finite grid, u_m^n converges in L2 to u^n at every grid point."]},
{text:"Use F001 and F002 on any fixed positive integer L with tau=T/L<=tau0. At each of its finitely many grid points pass to the limit in ||u_m(t_n)-u_m^n||_2<=C0 tau^gamma, and then take the maximum over n=0,...,L. This gives the asserted inequality for u0 with exactly the same constants. The argument works for every datum in the original ball, and the constants were selected before the datum and before the grid, using only T,gamma,R. In particular it proves the simultaneous uniform quantifiers in P, with no spatial cutoff in Psi. The distributional solution class and approximation identification are those of N001. This completes the full target.",local:["F001","F002"]}
